    panic in a function that cannot unwind
    (child aborted, signal)
no-panic-hook                ok     AK_INIT_NO_PANIC_HOOK: no line, and still an abort
    WANT: no line through the host sink, and still an abort
    (child aborted) panic in a function that cannot unwind
host-panic-own-hook          ok     a HOST panic is not the core's; two copies of std
    host hook fired 1 time(s); the CORE's sink got 0 line(s)
    # The core installed a hook and it did not fire, because the cdylib
    # carries its own `std`. That is not a defect: the hook is for panics
    # in the CORE, which is where the host cannot see them.
codec-after-init             ok     the codec works normally once initialised
    initialised by Ctx::new(); P1.2 encode 218121 B, prost 218121 B, equal=true, round trip=true
two-components               ok     two components, two flag sets: the first caller wins
    component A -> 0; component B -> -8, detail 2
    # THE FLAGS ARE A PROCESS-WIDE NEGOTIATION AND THE FIRST CALLER WINS.
    # Section 3 says a second call with different options fails, and treats
    # that as obviously right because the one-shot installs cannot be
    # redone. It is right, and it also means two independent components in
    # one process cannot both choose -- the second gets a hard failure for
    # asking, not the first caller's settings. Two hosts loading the same
    # core is the normal case for a shared library. Raised, not taken: the
    # alternatives (ignore the later flags, or merge them) each give up
    # something section 3 is deliberately buying.
init-races                   ok     8 threads calling ak_init at once: exactly one installs
    8 threads: 1 AK_OK, 7 AK_ALREADY_INITIALIZED, 0 other
